Z||000000||Ditz88GT||05-24-2000||05:50 PM||||I'm now thinking of doing the 3.2 conversion.  Since I will have it apart for a head gasket I figured I should just do it.  I think all I need is the 3.1 crank and have the block board .040 over.  I guess at that point I will also need new pistons.  Will I need connecting rods maybe a new cam, new ECM?  If any one has done this and has any advice it would be greatly appreciated.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||N_Man||05-24-2000||06:14 PM||||Eric has done it.<P>Im in the middle of it (only cause I couldn,t get my hands on a 3.4).  You will need crank, pistons, and a flywheel.  It is a straitfoward build.<P>Try a search youll find anything you need to know.<P>If your doing the rebuild and can get a 3.4 it IS the better way to go.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ditz88GT||05-25-2000||11:14 AM||||I wish I could install a 3.4, but I couldn't find one.  I don't think I will need the flywheel, my engine is an 88 and I think they were balanced, right?  Do I need the new connecting rods or can I use the ones from the 2.8?<p>[This message has been edited by Ditz88GT (edited 05-25-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||Phil||05-25-2000||11:42 AM||||Rods are the same -pistons are different||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ditz88GT||05-25-2000||11:48 AM||||Cool, will .040 over give me the 3.2?||||reg||5
Z||000005||Eric||05-25-2000||02:27 PM||||Tearing into the bottom end is a major deal.  And when you have to work with a machine shop, you should allow yourself a lot more time.  <P>My 88 flywheel worked just fine, but I did get it resurfaced for the new clutch.<P>You should put a new cam in it too.||||reg||1
